{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Axt2oV5bgGh3Qn6zRADezJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — contamcal-irt-calibration\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- **Primary: EMNLP 2026 ARR May 25** (D006: reinstated per user directive, 12 days)\n5\t- Fallback: AAAI 2027 (Jul-Aug 2026) / ICLR 2027 (Sep 2026)\n6\t- Track: Machine Learning for NLP / Resources and Evaluation\n7\t- Aligns with EMNLP 2026 special theme \"New Missions for NLP Research\" (explicitly invites contamination/data governance work)\n8\t\n9\t## Core Insight\n10\tWhen benchmark mitigation strategies systematically fail (Emperor's New Clothes, ICML 2025: 20 strategies tested, none effective), the principled alternative is post-hoc statistical calibration — analogous to causal inference's shift from eliminating confounders to statistically controlling them. ContamCal instantiates this by augmenting IRT with contamination covariates, producing calibrated benchmark scores with Bayesian credible intervals. Theoretically grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT tradition (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004), with 3 non-trivial adaptations for LLM evaluation.\n11\t\n12\t## Core Claims (3 条)\n13\t\n14\t1. **Calibration Claim**: ContamCal's contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model (with per-benchmark γ_k) recovers true (uncontaminated) benchmark performance with statistically significant MAE improvement over baselines and nominal credible interval coverage, validated on controlled SFT contamination injection across 3+ model scales (0.5B-7B) within the Qwen2.5 family and 4 benchmarks. (Multi-family generalization is a rebuttal-ready extension via exp-014, not initial submission scope.)\n15\t\n16\t2. **Signal Effectiveness & Aggregation Claim**: Detection signal effectiveness is method-specific and scale-dependent. Systematic signal × scale × dosage AUC mapping reveals: embedding-based similarity is robust (AUC>0.95 at d005/0.5B), while Min-K++ and Self-Critique fail at small model scales (AUC≈0.5 at 0.5B). Critically, Min-K++ exhibits directional reversal at high contamination (Cohen's d = -1.2 to -2.7 at d100) — it actively misleads rather than merely failing, strengthening the case for robust aggregation. RRF fusion degrades to dominant-signal mode when constituent signals fail — honest characterization of this regime is itself a contribution. Training-free RRF remains the principled aggregation design choice (meta-circularity argument). Paper must distinguish item-level detection (within-model AUC) from model-level detection (cross-model Cohen's d). (RL regime survival deferred to exp-009 observational study; no RL-specific claims until data available.)\n17\t   **C2 contingency (Reviewer WARNING)**: \"Scale-dependent\" is currently unverified — only 0.5B data exists. If 1.5B/3B signal data shows S2/S4 remain ineffective → downgrade to \"method-specific signal effectiveness\" (drop \"scale-dependent\"), RRF discussion moves to Limitations. Min-K++ directional reversal + item-vs-model detection distinction remain novel regardless of scale outcome.\n18\t\n19\t3. **Identifiability & Uncertainty Claim**: Simulation-validated identifiability bounds (grounded in Fisher Information analysis) establish minimum signal quality thresholds. ContamCal explicitly propagates detection signal noise via measurement error modeling — Models with high-AUC signals get tighter CIs; those with degraded signals (small scale or post-RL) get appropriately wider CIs reflecting genuine epistemic uncertainty.\n20\t\n21\t## Contributions (Hierarchical)\n22\t\n23\t### Core\n24\t1. ContamCal framework: two-stage pipeline (RRF provenance mapping → contamination-augmented 4PL IRT with per-benchmark γ_k and measurement error model for exposure) producing calibrated scores with credible intervals\n25\t2. Signal effectiveness characterization: systematic signal × scale × dosage AUC mapping revealing method-specific, scale-dependent signal reliability — an empirically novel finding with actionable guidance for the detection community (elevated from Supporting per Dream review; reframed from 'cliff-like RL degradation' per D022)\n26\t3. Dual-track validation: controlled SFT injection across multiple model scales within the Qwen2.5 family (internal validity) + ecological RL case study (external validity)\n27\t\n28\t### Supporting\n29\t4. Identifiability study: Fisher Information-grounded analysis + 144-condition simulation sweep establishing minimum AUC / model count thresholds\n30\t5. 7 baselines + 5 quantitative evaluation metrics + bootstrap CIs for contamination-aware calibration methodology\n31\t\n32\t## Story Spine\n33\t- **Problem**: Benchmark scores are increasingly unreliable due to training data contamination. Rephrased test items inflate MMLU accuracy by 8-15 points while evading n-gram decontamination. RL post-training may further complicate detection (under investigation, exp-009). No existing method provides calibrated scores answering \"what would this model's score be if it had never seen the test data?\"\n34\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Detection methods (Min-K%++, ConStat, Self-Critique, KDS) flag contamination but don't calibrate scores. IRT methods (PSN-IRT, Growing Pains, ATLAS, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024) evaluate models psychometrically but don't model contamination. DCR provides adjusted accuracy but lacks uncertainty bounds and RL handling. HiBayES provides Bayesian uncertainty but ignores contamination. Emperor's New Clothes (ICML 2025) demonstrates that all 20 mitigation strategies fail — motivating post-hoc calibration. Dynamic benchmarks (LiveBench) offer an alternative but require continuous curation.\n35\t- **Our framing**: ContamCal is a \"statistical safety net\" for when mitigation is unreliable. Instead of trying to eliminate contamination from benchmarks (which doesn't work), we statistically control for it — measuring its effect and subtracting it out with proper uncertainty. Grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004), with 3 non-trivial structural adaptations: (a) independent examinees → single model with joint item distribution (changes local independence semantics — see §Local Independence below), (b) binary leakage → continuous exposure scores (standard in explanatory IRT but requires noise propagation via measurement error model), (c) stable ability → regime-conditional ability (different training regimes and model scales require method-aware, scale-aware signal selection).\n36\t- **Headline result template**: \"On {benchmark}, ContamCal recovers true model performance within {X}pp MAE (bootstrap 95% CI: [{lo}, {hi}]) with {Y}% credible interval coverage, while naive scores overestimate by {Z} points under {W}% contamination dosage.\"\n37\t\n38\t## Venue Fit Risks\n39\t- **EMNLP (primary)**: Best venue fit — special theme explicitly invites contamination/data governance. Reviewers deeply familiar with NLP contamination literature (advantage: no need to motivate the problem heavily). Risk: reviewers may be less familiar with IRT/psychometric methods (mitigation: add intuitive explanations, visual pipeline overview, and connection to familiar NLP concepts like calibration/confidence estimation). Risk: may expect broader model coverage beyond Qwen2.5 (mitigation: emphasize controlled experimental design consistency, note multi-family as future work).\n40\t- **AAAI/ICLR (fallback)**: Strong fit for Bayesian/probabilistic methods. Reviewers may need more contamination context but appreciate methodological rigor.\n41\t- **Novelty positioning**: Core method is cross-disciplinary synthesis (detection signals + explanatory IRT + measurement error). NOT positioned as \"new algorithm\" — positioned as (a) signal effectiveness characterization as empirically novel finding, (b) identifiability bounds with theoretical grounding, (c) first framework bridging detection → calibration with proper UQ. RRF is a principled design choice (meta-circularity argument), not claimed as methodological contribution.\n42\t- **RL limitation**: RL models get wide CIs. This is honest — frame as \"calibration is only as good as its inputs.\" Expected regret quantification uses held-out clean benchmark as ground truth (NOT calibrated scores, to avoid circularity).\n43\t\n44\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Defenses\n45\t\n46\t| # | Concern | Defense | Experiment |\n47\t|---|---------|---------|------------|\n48\t| 1 | θ and γ identifiable? | Fisher Information analysis + 144-condition simulation sweep + MVP validation | exp-004 |\n49\t| 2 | Why not learned aggregation? | Meta-circularity: learned aggregator's training data may itself be contaminated; RRF is design choice, not contribution | exp-003 |\n50\t| 3 | SFT injection ≠ real contamination | Dual-track: controlled SFT (internal) + ecological RL (external) + near-zero γ check (specificity). SFT is \"best-available proxy\", acknowledged as limitation | exp-006, exp-009 |\n51\t| 4 | Scale limited to 3B | 7B controlled + 72B observational | exp-007, exp-012 |\n52\t| 5 | Why 4PL not 2PL? | MVP: 2PL γ=0.16 vs 4PL γ=0.34 at N=100; c/d necessary for realistic item modeling | exp-006 |\n53\t| 6 | Too few models (50)? | Few-examinee study (N=20→200) + Growing Pains pre-estimation | exp-004 (subsumes) |\n54\t| 7 | Noisy exposure scores | Measurement error model (exposure as noisy observation of latent τ) propagates uncertainty into posteriors automatically | exp-005 |\n55\t| 8 | No baselines | 7 baselines including DCR (EMNLP 2025) + bootstrap CIs on all comparisons | exp-006 |\n56\t| 9 | Single global γ misspecified? | Per-benchmark γ_k is primary (natural in per-benchmark fitting); joint model with shared global γ is ablation. Report both to show contamination effect heterogeneity across benchmarks | exp-006 |\n57\t| 10 | Local independence violated? | Discussed explicitly; single model responds to all items → item responses correlated through shared parameters. Empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations. This is a known IRT limitation when N_examinees is small. | exp-006 |\n58\t| 11 | Single model family? | Acknowledged as limitation. Qwen2.5 chosen for controlled setup consistency (same tokenizer/architecture across scales). Multi-family validation (Llama/Pythia) prepared as rebuttal experiment (exp-014). | exp-014 (rebuttal) |\n59\t| 12 | RL regret circular? | Ground truth for regret = held-out clean benchmark scores, NOT calibrated scores. Explicitly stated | exp-009 |\n60\t| 13 | S2/S4 fail at 0.5B — RRF is just S1? | Scale-dependent: honestly report single-signal regime at 0.5B. Verify S2/S4 at 1.5B/3B. RRF value = principled aggregation when signals ARE available + graceful degradation when not. | exp-008, exp-002 |\n61\t| 14 | d100 AUC=0.5 as signal failure? | Labeling artifact: d100 contaminates ALL items → labels lose discriminative power. Must NOT be interpreted as signal degradation. Present only d005/d025 for signal evaluation. | exp-008 |\n62\t\n63\t## Evaluation Protocol (5 Metrics)\n64\t1. **Calibration error**: MAE between calibrated score and ground-truth clean accuracy, with bootstrap 95% CIs\n65\t2. **Interval coverage**: Fraction of true values within 50%/80%/95% credible intervals\n66\t3. **Interval sharpness**: Average CI width (tighter is better at fixed coverage)\n67\t4. **Ranking preservation**: Kendall's τ between calibrated and ground-truth model rankings\n68\t5. **Dose-response monotonicity**: γ effect increases monotonically with contamination dosage\n69\t\n70\t## Benchmarks (3 main + 1 appendix)\n71\t1. **MMLU** — knowledge-intensive multiple-choice (14K items)\n72\t2. **GSM8K** — mathematical reasoning free-form (~1.3K items)\n73\t3. **ARC-Challenge** — science reasoning multiple-choice\n74\t4. **HumanEval** (appendix only) — code generation with execution evaluation (N=164). Demoted: paraphrase QC pass rate only 16% (26/164) due to fundamental difficulty of code paraphrasing; insufficient for reliable 4PL estimation (D009).\n75\t\n76\t## Baselines (7)\n77\t0. **Raw (no adjustment)** — report original benchmark scores as anchor\n78\t1. **Remove-and-rescore** — drop flagged items, rescore on remaining\n79\t2. **Single-signal 2PL+γ** — best single detection signal + simpler IRT\n80\t3. **Vanilla 4PL** — standard IRT without contamination covariate (γ=0)\n81\t4. **Linear exposure regression + bootstrap CI** — simplest post-hoc adjustment (tests if IRT complexity is necessary)\n82\t5. **Emperor's mitigation strategies** — rephrasing + option shuffling (2 representative strategies from ICML 2025)\n83\t6. **DCR fuzzy inference (EMNLP 2025)** — the only existing method producing adjusted accuracy; direct predecessor, must compare\n84\t\n85\t## Local Independence Discussion\n86\tStandard IRT assumes independent examinees. In LLM evaluation, a single model produces all item responses — responses are correlated through shared model parameters. This fundamentally alters local independence. Our approach: (1) fit IRT per-benchmark (not jointly across benchmarks) to reduce correlation scope, (2) empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations, (3) discuss as known limitation with reference to fixed-effects IRT literature (Mislevy 1986). The coverage gap (if observed) may partly reflect this violation — measurement error model for exposure partially compensates by widening CIs.\n87\t\n88\t## Required Figures (Dream D2: \"zero figures\" flagged as serious deficiency)\n89\t1. Pipeline overview diagram (2-stage: RRF → 4PL+γ IRT)\n90\t2. Signal × scale × dosage AUC heatmap (exp-008) — 'regime' axis replaced by scale+dosage per D022\n91\t3. Dose-response curves (contamination dosage vs accuracy inflation, exp-001/006)\n92\t4. Identifiability phase diagram (AUC × N_models → γ recoverability, exp-004)\n93\t5. Calibrated-vs-true accuracy scatter with CIs (exp-006)\n94\t\n95\t## Citation Requirements (35+ minimum, expanded per Dream D2)\n96\tMust include: Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017, De Boeck & Wilson 2004, Adams et al. 1997, San Martín et al. 2009, Maris & Bechger 2009, Cormack et al. 2009 (RRF), Mislevy 1986, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024, LiveBench, TinyBenchmarks (Polo et al. 2024), KDS, CDD, contamination surveys, SBC (Talts et al. 2018), HiBayES, plus all signals/methods cited in IDEA_BRIEF.\n97\tDream D2 additions (all 3 tiers flagged):\n98\t- DIF literature: Holland & Wainer 1993, Thissen et al. 1993, Zumbo 1999 (γ_k is essentially DIF between exposure groups)\n99\t- ML calibration: Guo et al. 2017, Platt 1999 (disambiguate \"calibration\" — ours is decontamination, not probability calibration)\n100\t- Conformal prediction: Angelopoulos & Bates 2023 (alternative UQ with distribution-free coverage guarantees; discuss why Bayesian IRT preferred)\n101\t- Data attribution: Pruthi et al. 2020 (TracIn), Park et al. 2023 (TRAK) — potential additional signals, mention in Future Work\n102\t- Multidimensional IRT: Reckase 2009 (acknowledge unidimensional θ for MMLU as simplification in Limitations)\n103\t\n104\t## Fisher Information Derivation (Dream D2: all 3 tiers flagged as critical gap)\n105\tMust derive I(γ) for 4PL+γ model in appendix. Currently stated without proof.\n106\tThe derivation must show: (1) relationship between signal AUC and γ recoverability, (2) approximation assumptions, (3) how measurement error factors in.\n107\tThis is CPU-only theoretical work — can be done in parallel with GPU experiments.\n108\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5f0cff07-cb6a-487f-be6b-6af3047fe23a","uuid":"2dd06dee-1c52-47ba-b792-f4b36f9999d4","timestamp":"2026-05-14T18:03:44.684Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_13/projects/contamcal-irt-calibration/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T171536/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — contamcal-irt-calibration\n\n## Target Venue\n- **Primary: EMNLP 2026 ARR May 25** (D006: reinstated per user directive, 12 days)\n- Fallback: AAAI 2027 (Jul-Aug 2026) / ICLR 2027 (Sep 2026)\n- Track: Machine Learning for NLP / Resources and Evaluation\n- Aligns with EMNLP 2026 special theme \"New Missions for NLP Research\" (explicitly invites contamination/data governance work)\n\n## Core Insight\nWhen benchmark mitigation strategies systematically fail (Emperor's New Clothes, ICML 2025: 20 strategies tested, none effective), the principled alternative is post-hoc statistical calibration — analogous to causal inference's shift from eliminating confounders to statistically controlling them. ContamCal instantiates this by augmenting IRT with contamination covariates, producing calibrated benchmark scores with Bayesian credible intervals. Theoretically grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT tradition (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004), with 3 non-trivial adaptations for LLM evaluation.\n\n## Core Claims (3 条)\n\n1. **Calibration Claim**: ContamCal's contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model (with per-benchmark γ_k) recovers true (uncontaminated) benchmark performance with statistically significant MAE improvement over baselines and nominal credible interval coverage, validated on controlled SFT contamination injection across 3+ model scales (0.5B-7B) within the Qwen2.5 family and 4 benchmarks. (Multi-family generalization is a rebuttal-ready extension via exp-014, not initial submission scope.)\n\n2. **Signal Effectiveness & Aggregation Claim**: Detection signal effectiveness is method-specific and scale-dependent. Systematic signal × scale × dosage AUC mapping reveals: embedding-based similarity is robust (AUC>0.95 at d005/0.5B), while Min-K++ and Self-Critique fail at small model scales (AUC≈0.5 at 0.5B). Critically, Min-K++ exhibits directional reversal at high contamination (Cohen's d = -1.2 to -2.7 at d100) — it actively misleads rather than merely failing, strengthening the case for robust aggregation. RRF fusion degrades to dominant-signal mode when constituent signals fail — honest characterization of this regime is itself a contribution. Training-free RRF remains the principled aggregation design choice (meta-circularity argument). Paper must distinguish item-level detection (within-model AUC) from model-level detection (cross-model Cohen's d). (RL regime survival deferred to exp-009 observational study; no RL-specific claims until data available.)\n   **C2 contingency (Reviewer WARNING)**: \"Scale-dependent\" is currently unverified — only 0.5B data exists. If 1.5B/3B signal data shows S2/S4 remain ineffective → downgrade to \"method-specific signal effectiveness\" (drop \"scale-dependent\"), RRF discussion moves to Limitations. Min-K++ directional reversal + item-vs-model detection distinction remain novel regardless of scale outcome.\n\n3. **Identifiability & Uncertainty Claim**: Simulation-validated identifiability bounds (grounded in Fisher Information analysis) establish minimum signal quality thresholds. ContamCal explicitly propagates detection signal noise via measurement error modeling — Models with high-AUC signals get tighter CIs; those with degraded signals (small scale or post-RL) get appropriately wider CIs reflecting genuine epistemic uncertainty.\n\n## Contributions (Hierarchical)\n\n### Core\n1. ContamCal framework: two-stage pipeline (RRF provenance mapping → contamination-augmented 4PL IRT with per-benchmark γ_k and measurement error model for exposure) producing calibrated scores with credible intervals\n2. Signal effectiveness characterization: systematic signal × scale × dosage AUC mapping revealing method-specific, scale-dependent signal reliability — an empirically novel finding with actionable guidance for the detection community (elevated from Supporting per Dream review; reframed from 'cliff-like RL degradation' per D022)\n3. Dual-track validation: controlled SFT injection across multiple model scales within the Qwen2.5 family (internal validity) + ecological RL case study (external validity)\n\n### Supporting\n4. Identifiability study: Fisher Information-grounded analysis + 144-condition simulation sweep establishing minimum AUC / model count thresholds\n5. 7 baselines + 5 quantitative evaluation metrics + bootstrap CIs for contamination-aware calibration methodology\n\n## Story Spine\n- **Problem**: Benchmark scores are increasingly unreliable due to training data contamination. Rephrased test items inflate MMLU accuracy by 8-15 points while evading n-gram decontamination. RL post-training may further complicate detection (under investigation, exp-009). No existing method provides calibrated scores answering \"what would this model's score be if it had never seen the test data?\"\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Detection methods (Min-K%++, ConStat, Self-Critique, KDS) flag contamination but don't calibrate scores. IRT methods (PSN-IRT, Growing Pains, ATLAS, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024) evaluate models psychometrically but don't model contamination. DCR provides adjusted accuracy but lacks uncertainty bounds and RL handling. HiBayES provides Bayesian uncertainty but ignores contamination. Emperor's New Clothes (ICML 2025) demonstrates that all 20 mitigation strategies fail — motivating post-hoc calibration. Dynamic benchmarks (LiveBench) offer an alternative but require continuous curation.\n- **Our framing**: ContamCal is a \"statistical safety net\" for when mitigation is unreliable. Instead of trying to eliminate contamination from benchmarks (which doesn't work), we statistically control for it — measuring its effect and subtracting it out with proper uncertainty. Grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004), with 3 non-trivial structural adaptations: (a) independent examinees → single model with joint item distribution (changes local independence semantics — see §Local Independence below), (b) binary leakage → continuous exposure scores (standard in explanatory IRT but requires noise propagation via measurement error model), (c) stable ability → regime-conditional ability (different training regimes and model scales require method-aware, scale-aware signal selection).\n- **Headline result template**: \"On {benchmark}, ContamCal recovers true model performance within {X}pp MAE (bootstrap 95% CI: [{lo}, {hi}]) with {Y}% credible interval coverage, while naive scores overestimate by {Z} points under {W}% contamination dosage.\"\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n- **EMNLP (primary)**: Best venue fit — special theme explicitly invites contamination/data governance. Reviewers deeply familiar with NLP contamination literature (advantage: no need to motivate the problem heavily). Risk: reviewers may be less familiar with IRT/psychometric methods (mitigation: add intuitive explanations, visual pipeline overview, and connection to familiar NLP concepts like calibration/confidence estimation). Risk: may expect broader model coverage beyond Qwen2.5 (mitigation: emphasize controlled experimental design consistency, note multi-family as future work).\n- **AAAI/ICLR (fallback)**: Strong fit for Bayesian/probabilistic methods. Reviewers may need more contamination context but appreciate methodological rigor.\n- **Novelty positioning**: Core method is cross-disciplinary synthesis (detection signals + explanatory IRT + measurement error). NOT positioned as \"new algorithm\" — positioned as (a) signal effectiveness characterization as empirically novel finding, (b) identifiability bounds with theoretical grounding, (c) first framework bridging detection → calibration with proper UQ. RRF is a principled design choice (meta-circularity argument), not claimed as methodological contribution.\n- **RL limitation**: RL models get wide CIs. This is honest — frame as \"calibration is only as good as its inputs.\" Expected regret quantification uses held-out clean benchmark as ground truth (NOT calibrated scores, to avoid circularity).\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Defenses\n\n| # | Concern | Defense | Experiment |\n|---|---------|---------|------------|\n| 1 | θ and γ identifiable? | Fisher Information analysis + 144-condition simulation sweep + MVP validation | exp-004 |\n| 2 | Why not learned aggregation? | Meta-circularity: learned aggregator's training data may itself be contaminated; RRF is design choice, not contribution | exp-003 |\n| 3 | SFT injection ≠ real contamination | Dual-track: controlled SFT (internal) + ecological RL (external) + near-zero γ check (specificity). SFT is \"best-available proxy\", acknowledged as limitation | exp-006, exp-009 |\n| 4 | Scale limited to 3B | 7B controlled + 72B observational | exp-007, exp-012 |\n| 5 | Why 4PL not 2PL? | MVP: 2PL γ=0.16 vs 4PL γ=0.34 at N=100; c/d necessary for realistic item modeling | exp-006 |\n| 6 | Too few models (50)? | Few-examinee study (N=20→200) + Growing Pains pre-estimation | exp-004 (subsumes) |\n| 7 | Noisy exposure scores | Measurement error model (exposure as noisy observation of latent τ) propagates uncertainty into posteriors automatically | exp-005 |\n| 8 | No baselines | 7 baselines including DCR (EMNLP 2025) + bootstrap CIs on all comparisons | exp-006 |\n| 9 | Single global γ misspecified? | Per-benchmark γ_k is primary (natural in per-benchmark fitting); joint model with shared global γ is ablation. Report both to show contamination effect heterogeneity across benchmarks | exp-006 |\n| 10 | Local independence violated? | Discussed explicitly; single model responds to all items → item responses correlated through shared parameters. Empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations. This is a known IRT limitation when N_examinees is small. | exp-006 |\n| 11 | Single model family? | Acknowledged as limitation. Qwen2.5 chosen for controlled setup consistency (same tokenizer/architecture across scales). Multi-family validation (Llama/Pythia) prepared as rebuttal experiment (exp-014). | exp-014 (rebuttal) |\n| 12 | RL regret circular? | Ground truth for regret = held-out clean benchmark scores, NOT calibrated scores. Explicitly stated | exp-009 |\n| 13 | S2/S4 fail at 0.5B — RRF is just S1? | Scale-dependent: honestly report single-signal regime at 0.5B. Verify S2/S4 at 1.5B/3B. RRF value = principled aggregation when signals ARE available + graceful degradation when not. | exp-008, exp-002 |\n| 14 | d100 AUC=0.5 as signal failure? | Labeling artifact: d100 contaminates ALL items → labels lose discriminative power. Must NOT be interpreted as signal degradation. Present only d005/d025 for signal evaluation. | exp-008 |\n\n## Evaluation Protocol (5 Metrics)\n1. **Calibration error**: MAE between calibrated score and ground-truth clean accuracy, with bootstrap 95% CIs\n2. **Interval coverage**: Fraction of true values within 50%/80%/95% credible intervals\n3. **Interval sharpness**: Average CI width (tighter is better at fixed coverage)\n4. **Ranking preservation**: Kendall's τ between calibrated and ground-truth model rankings\n5. **Dose-response monotonicity**: γ effect increases monotonically with contamination dosage\n\n## Benchmarks (3 main + 1 appendix)\n1. **MMLU** — knowledge-intensive multiple-choice (14K items)\n2. **GSM8K** — mathematical reasoning free-form (~1.3K items)\n3. **ARC-Challenge** — science reasoning multiple-choice\n4. **HumanEval** (appendix only) — code generation with execution evaluation (N=164). Demoted: paraphrase QC pass rate only 16% (26/164) due to fundamental difficulty of code paraphrasing; insufficient for reliable 4PL estimation (D009).\n\n## Baselines (7)\n0. **Raw (no adjustment)** — report original benchmark scores as anchor\n1. **Remove-and-rescore** — drop flagged items, rescore on remaining\n2. **Single-signal 2PL+γ** — best single detection signal + simpler IRT\n3. **Vanilla 4PL** — standard IRT without contamination covariate (γ=0)\n4. **Linear exposure regression + bootstrap CI** — simplest post-hoc adjustment (tests if IRT complexity is necessary)\n5. **Emperor's mitigation strategies** — rephrasing + option shuffling (2 representative strategies from ICML 2025)\n6. **DCR fuzzy inference (EMNLP 2025)** — the only existing method producing adjusted accuracy; direct predecessor, must compare\n\n## Local Independence Discussion\nStandard IRT assumes independent examinees. In LLM evaluation, a single model produces all item responses — responses are correlated through shared model parameters. This fundamentally alters local independence. Our approach: (1) fit IRT per-benchmark (not jointly across benchmarks) to reduce correlation scope, (2) empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations, (3) discuss as known limitation with reference to fixed-effects IRT literature (Mislevy 1986). The coverage gap (if observed) may partly reflect this violation — measurement error model for exposure partially compensates by widening CIs.\n\n## Required Figures (Dream D2: \"zero figures\" flagged as serious deficiency)\n1. Pipeline overview diagram (2-stage: RRF → 4PL+γ IRT)\n2. Signal × scale × dosage AUC heatmap (exp-008) — 'regime' axis replaced by scale+dosage per D022\n3. Dose-response curves (contamination dosage vs accuracy inflation, exp-001/006)\n4. Identifiability phase diagram (AUC × N_models → γ recoverability, exp-004)\n5. Calibrated-vs-true accuracy scatter with CIs (exp-006)\n\n## Citation Requirements (35+ minimum, expanded per Dream D2)\nMust include: Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017, De Boeck & Wilson 2004, Adams et al. 1997, San Martín et al. 2009, Maris & Bechger 2009, Cormack et al. 2009 (RRF), Mislevy 1986, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024, LiveBench, TinyBenchmarks (Polo et al. 2024), KDS, CDD, contamination surveys, SBC (Talts et al. 2018), HiBayES, plus all signals/methods cited in IDEA_BRIEF.\nDream D2 additions (all 3 tiers flagged):\n- DIF literature: Holland & Wainer 1993, Thissen et al. 1993, Zumbo 1999 (γ_k is essentially DIF between exposure groups)\n- ML calibration: Guo et al. 2017, Platt 1999 (disambiguate \"calibration\" — ours is decontamination, not probability calibration)\n- Conformal prediction: Angelopoulos & Bates 2023 (alternative UQ with distribution-free coverage guarantees; discuss why Bayesian IRT preferred)\n- Data attribution: Pruthi et al. 2020 (TracIn), Park et al. 2023 (TRAK) — potential additional signals, mention in Future Work\n- Multidimensional IRT: Reckase 2009 (acknowledge unidimensional θ for MMLU as simplification in Limitations)\n\n## Fisher Information Derivation (Dream D2: all 3 tiers flagged as critical gap)\nMust derive I(γ) for 4PL+γ model in appendix. Currently stated without proof.\nThe derivation must show: (1) relationship between signal AUC and γ recoverability, (2) approximation assumptions, (3) how measurement error factors in.\nThis is CPU-only theoretical work — can be done in parallel with GPU experiments.\n","numLines":108,"startLine":1,"totalLines":108}}}
